WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.360
For about sixty years, insulin for people with diabetes came mostly from the

2
00:00:08.360 --> 00:00:10.020
pancreases of cattle and pigs.

3
00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:15.080
In 1982, the FDA approved human insulin made by bacteria.

4
00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:18.180
Bacteria have no human insulin gene.

5
00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:21.360
So how does a human gene get into a microbe?

6
00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:25.120
And can we design life, and how should we decide when we should?

7
00:00:26.133 --> 00:00:31.433
Genetic engineering means using laboratory tools to change an organism's DNA.

8
00:00:32.433 --> 00:00:37.713
DNA is the same kind of molecule in every living thing, and nearly all living things

9
00:00:37.713 --> 00:00:42.413
use the same genetic code, so a cell can often read a gene from another species.

10
00:00:43.333 --> 00:00:48.613
A restriction enzyme is a protein from bacteria that cuts DNA at one particular

11
00:00:48.613 --> 00:00:54.073
short sequence. Many restriction enzymes leave short single-stranded tails, called

12
00:00:54.073 --> 00:00:55.073
sticky ends.

13
00:00:55.767 --> 00:01:00.647
A plasmid is a small, usually circular piece of DNA in a bacterium.

14
00:01:01.367 --> 00:01:03.707
It copies itself apart from the main chromosome.

15
00:01:04.587 --> 00:01:08.627
Cut it open with the same enzyme that cut the gene, and the sticky ends match.

16
00:01:09.647 --> 00:01:13.827
DNA ligase is an enzyme that joins two DNA ends into one strand.

17
00:01:13.827 --> 00:01:18.607
The result is recombinant DNA: DNA from more than one source.

18
00:01:19.650 --> 00:01:21.550
Put the plasmids into bacteria.

19
00:01:22.490 --> 00:01:24.150
Only some bacteria take one up.

20
00:01:24.850 --> 00:01:28.590
The plasmid usually carries a gene for resistance to an antibiotic.

21
00:01:29.630 --> 00:01:34.390
So growing the bacteria with that antibiotic leaves mostly the ones that took it.

22
00:01:35.070 --> 00:01:37.830
As they divide, the plasmid is copied, gene and all.

23
00:01:38.630 --> 00:01:42.810
If the gene is switched on, they make each insulin chain attached to a bacterial

24
00:01:42.810 --> 00:01:46.550
protein. The chains are cut free, purified and joined.

25
00:01:46.550 --> 00:01:46.830
If you enjoyed this video, please like and subscribe.

26
00:01:46.830 --> 00:01:47.583
Thanks for watching.

27
00:01:47.583 --> 00:01:53.143
The polymerase chain reaction, or PCR, makes many copies of one chosen stretch of

28
00:01:53.143 --> 00:01:57.323
DNA. In an ideal reaction, the amount doubles each cycle.

29
00:01:58.363 --> 00:02:01.243
Gel electrophoresis sorts DNA pieces by size.

30
00:02:02.223 --> 00:02:03.543
Shorter pieces travel farther.

31
00:02:04.483 --> 00:02:10.043
In the most common version, CRISPR uses a guide RNA that matches one sequence, and a

32
00:02:10.043 --> 00:02:12.763
protein called Cas9 cuts both strands there.

33
00:02:13.717 --> 00:02:15.177
So can we design life?

34
00:02:16.017 --> 00:02:18.037
With these tools, people can change DNA.

35
00:02:19.077 --> 00:02:23.777
Bacteria make human insulin because they copy a plasmid and read the human gene on

36
00:02:23.777 --> 00:02:28.757
it. Related tools now change crops, and CRISPR now edits blood stem cells.

37
00:02:29.657 --> 00:02:34.537
Whether a change is worth making is judged use by use, by evidence and by values

38
00:02:34.537 --> 00:02:36.957
such as safety, fair access and limits.
